{"uid":"4e97420f1efbd4c3","name":"test_pages_never_repeat_or_skip_a_product[rating_desc]","fullName":"tests.api.tests.test_catalog.TestPagination#test_pages_never_repeat_or_skip_a_product","historyId":"c2ad44c7a4e2d14d8b444792f95febf0","time":{"start":1787980943688,"stop":1787980943803,"duration":115},"description":"The reason every sort carries a unique tiebreaker.\n\n        Ordering by a non-unique key alone lets PostgreSQL return equal-valued\n        rows in any order it likes, so page 2 can repeat a row already shown on\n        page 1 - and silently omit another.\n\n        The products are created by this test, all with **identical** price,\n        rating and name prefix, and are isolated behind a unique brand. That\n        does two things: it forces the tie case the tiebreaker exists for, and\n        it makes the walk immune to other workers creating products mid-run,\n        which is what made an earlier version of this test fail under `-n auto`.\n        ","descriptionHtml":"<p>The reason every sort carries a unique tiebreaker.</p>\n<pre><code>    Ordering by a non-unique key alone lets PostgreSQL return equal-valued\n    rows in any order it likes, so page 2 can repeat a row already shown on\n    page 1 - and silently omit another.\n\n    The products are created by this test, all with **identical** price,\n    rating and name prefix, and are isolated behind a unique brand. That\n    does two things: it forces the tie case the tiebreaker exists for, and\n    it makes the walk immune to other workers creating products mid-run,\n    which is what made an earlier version of this test fail under `-n auto`.\n</code></pre>\n","status":"passed","flaky":false,"newFailed":false,"newBroken":false,"newPassed":false,"retriesCount":0,"retriesStatusChange":false,"beforeStages":[{"name":"_session_faker","time":{"start":1787980939070,"stop":1787980939071,"duration":1},"status":"passed","steps":[],"attachments":[],"parameters":[],"shouldDisplayMessage":false,"stepsCo